/* Exam 3 "Hear the why": scripts added in the final audio review (her quiz items, Why items, new items). Loaded after narration3_new.js. */
Object.assign(NARR_Q, {
"GAP3-033": "Jeanna Marraffa, Victor Cohen and Mary Ann Howland wrote the primer on antidotes for toxicological emergencies, published in the American Journal of Health-System Pharmacy in two thousand twelve. She recommended it with DiPiro chapter e nine as a source on treating specific poisonings. In class she called the Marraffa article a really good article that breaks down step by step the symptoms of these overdoses, the treatments, and importantly their mechanisms of action, and said it is quicker to read than the DiPiro chapter. Her poisoning table key cites the Marraffa article as the reference for most rows. The other author names belong to the other readings. Bryan Hayes and Natalija Farrell wrote DiPiro chapter e nine, Clinical Toxicology. Kent Olson and colleagues wrote the American Association of Poison Control Centers guideline on out-of-hospital management of calcium channel blocker ingestion, and Wax and colleagues wrote the matching beta-blocker guideline, both in two thousand five. Rodriguez-Mozaz and Weinberg wrote the report on pharmaceuticals in water.",
"GAP3-034": "A regional poison control center is reached at one eight hundred, two two two, one two two two. The number is printed in the Marraffa antidotes article; she did not say it in class. The article says that because recommendations may change, clinicians should always consult a regional poison control center for the most current recommendations on antidote use, and to report exposures and poisonings. Her guideline key sends patients to the same place. For an asymptomatic patient found after the window for the product, such as six hours after an immediate-release product, her key says to call the Poison Center: they can probably be managed at home, and the Poison Center will inform you of follow-up procedures. In class she said that with the person at home you call the poison center and nine one one, and you do not make them vomit. The wrong numbers in the bank rearrange the same digits: one eight hundred, two two two, two one two two; one eight hundred, one two two, one two two two; and one eight eight eight, two two two, one two two two. Only the real number has eight hundred, then two two two, then one two two two.",
"GRG3-024": "Inside the window an asymptomatic patient goes to a hospital; after it, call the Poison Center, and the patient can probably be managed at home. An immediate-release product has a six hour window for both classes, beta blockers except sotalol. A sustained-release beta blocker has eight hours. A modified-release calcium channel blocker other than verapamil has eighteen hours. Modified-release verapamil has twenty four hours, because it has multiple mechanisms. Immediate-release products are absorbed rapidly; extended-release products form bezoars that may break apart at unpredictable times, so their windows are longer. The trap is swapping eight and eighteen: eight belongs to beta blockers, and eighteen to calcium channel blockers.",
"L13-047": "Red touching yellow marks a coral snake. In class she gave the rhyme: red and yellow kills a fellow, so if the red touches the yellow, the snake is poisonous. The king snake is the look-alike. It also has red, yellow and black bands, but in a different order, so the red touches the black: red and black, friend of Jack. The king snake is not poisonous, and people keep them as pets, so the king snake is wrong here. The coral snake is very venomous, its venom carries a neurotoxin, and it lives in Texas, especially East Texas, under rocks, logs and leaves. The rattlesnake and the water moccasin are wrong because they are the fanged snakes she contrasted with the coral snake; the banding rhyme is only about the coral snake and the king snake. Because the snake makes the venom, the venom is a toxin. She also said not to kill a snake with these colors unless it has been verified as a coral snake, so the harmless snakes stay around.",
"L13-048": "The coral snake's venom carries a neurotoxin. In class she said the coral snake is very venomous and has a neurotoxin, and that it lives in Texas, especially East Texas. Because the snake makes the venom, it is a toxin, a substance from a biological system, not a toxicant. The coral snake has a small head and is not a viper, so instead of striking with fangs like a rattlesnake or a water moccasin, it bites and chews, often between fingers or toes, to inject the venom. Its look-alike, the king snake, with red touching black, is not poisonous. A hepatotoxin is wrong; her liver-toxin example was the acetaminophen metabolite N A P Q I. A carcinogen is wrong; a snakebite is a single, immediate exposure, and she named a neurotoxin. A toxicant is wrong, because a toxicant comes from human activity and venom is made by the snake.",
"L13-049": "Compound B first reaches the toxic range at about the fourth or fifth dose. In the repeated-dose panel, each dose is one quarter of the single dose, given at the same interval to all three compounds, and a concentration above two is toxic. B's half-life is about equal to the dosing frequency, about one day on the caption, so roughly half is cleared between doses and its peaks climb slowly. The figure stops before B's line clearly enters the toxic zone. In class she said the figure was not drawn very well, but if you extrapolate, maybe at the fourth dose you are in the toxic zone, and at the fifth for sure. The first dose is wrong, because a quarter dose sits well below the toxic range for every compound. The second dose is wrong, because B's peak is still below the toxic range. The third dose is wrong, because that is compound A, with the longest half-life, which barely declines between doses. C, cleared before each next dose, never reaches the toxic range.",
"L13-101": "The coral snake bites and chews because it has a small head and is not a viper. In class she pointed to its little bitty head and said it has a little piano hinge mouth, so it bites and chews, like between the fingers or between the toes, and injects the venom that way. Rattlesnakes and water moccasins, which also live in Texas, have fangs. Weak venom is wrong, because she called the coral snake very venomous, with a neurotoxin; the chewing comes from its head and mouth, not from its venom. Calling it a viper like the rattlesnake is wrong, because she said plainly that it is not a viper. Living under rocks, logs and leaves is true of the coral snake, but where it lives does not explain how it bites. Its venom is a toxin because the snake makes it. The red-touches-yellow banding tells it apart from the harmless king snake.",
"L13-102": "A medication counts as a xenobiotic because it is not made by the body or expected to be in it. In class she said a xenobiotic is any compound that gets into an organism that is not normally produced by that organism or expected to be present in it. Harm is not part of the definition, so a medication at a safe dose still qualifies. She listed what a xenobiotic could be: something poisonous, a toxin or a toxicant, a food additive, which could be a toxicant, or a medication, which could be poisonous. The other choices are other definitions. A toxic substance made by a living thing is a toxin, such as snake venom. A toxic by-product of human activity is a toxicant, such as a smokestack emission. Anything that can cause a deleterious response is a poison, the general lay term. Toxin and toxicant describe origin, poison describes harm, and xenobiotic describes only being foreign to the organism.",
"L13-103": "A toxicologist cannot judge the vapor from the photograph because it could just be steam until someone samples it. In class she asked whether the vapor from the smokestacks is toxic, poisonous or harmful, and answered that it could be, but it could just be steam. Someone would have to go in, sample that vapor and find out what it is, and people do that all the time. If the vapor does carry something harmful, that substance, made by human activity, would be a toxicant. So the choice saying toxicants come from biological systems is wrong, because that describes toxins. The claim that inhaled vapors do not reach toxic doses is wrong, because inhalation is one of the major routes of exposure, second only to intravenous in effectiveness. The claim that toxicity depends on the route is wrong as an answer here, because route is only one factor of exposure, and first one has to know what the substance is.",
"L13-104": "The staff have to be careful because the contaminant on the patient can expose them too. This is an occupational exposure, a workplace exposure, and healthcare workers have them. In class she said that when someone comes into the emergency room covered with a compound, for example splattered with an organophosphate, people in the emergency room have to be very careful not to get exposed to the contaminant that person was hit with. She added that pharmacists too may have a risk of occupational exposure, and her other healthcare examples were chemotherapy mixed in a hood with the wrong airflow, and radiation. Infection by needle stick is wrong, because in this course she set pathogens and needle sticks aside. Calling it environmental for the staff is wrong, because an exposure at work is occupational; environmental exposure is pollution in the surroundings, such as bus fumes or landfill dust. The patient's own medications are wrong, because medications are a source for the patient taking them, not the hazard here.",
"L13-105": "Parenteral exposure is seen much more in healthcare because medications are given by injection and drug abuse exposes people to injected substances. The major routes of exposure are oral, the most common, then inhalation, topical and parenteral. In class she said that healthcare sees a lot more parenteral exposure than an occupational setting, through drug abuse exposing people to damaging substances and through giving medications intravenously or by some other injection, and she called it a major route of exposure in healthcare. Intravenous dosing is also the most effective route, ahead of inhalation, oral and dermal. The claim that oral exposure does not happen in healthcare is wrong; oral is the most common route overall, and she did not exclude it. The claim that intravenous dosing is the least effective route is reversed; she ranked it most effective. The claim that parenteral doses go through first-pass metabolism is reversed too; an intravenous dose skips first pass, and an oral dose goes to the liver first.",
"L13-106": "A person's exposure duration is hard to classify because people often do not realize they are being exposed, or cannot remember when it began. In an animal study the length of exposure is set by design, so it is known. In class she said that you probably do not realize you are being exposed to something, or you cannot remember when your workplace started to have a funny smell, whether a couple of days ago or two months ago. That is common with occupational and environmental exposures, such as a pollutant or an occupational leak. The claim that humans are exposed acutely but not chronically is wrong; she asked the room and answered yes to both. The claim that pollutants are cleared before a duration can be set is wrong, because clearance was not her reason. The claim that the route of a workplace leak is unknown is wrong, because route is a separate factor of exposure; the duration problem is not knowing when, or whether, the exposure began.",
"L13-107": "Toxicologists still classify a human exposure so they can link the person's response to its duration. In animal studies, acute is less than twenty four hours, subacute is less than one month, subchronic is one to three months, and chronic is more than three months, up to years. In class she said that even for humans, toxicologists try to define exposures by these definitions, so they can categorize the exposure and observe what kind of response the person is having based on that duration of exposure. That holds even when the person cannot say exactly when the exposure started. Working out the route is wrong, because route is a separate factor of exposure and the duration category does not reveal it. Calculating an L D fifty is wrong, because the L D fifty comes from population dose-response studies in animals, not from one person's exposure. Deciding toxin or toxicant is wrong, because that depends on the origin of the substance, biological or man-made, not on how long the exposure lasted.",
"L13-108": "Linking diethylstilbestrol, called D E S, to the cancer took a lot of medical epidemiology because the daughters developed it about twenty years later. D E S is an estrogen given in the nineteen fifties to help women keep a pregnancy when they were having miscarriages. The mothers might get a little nausea, because it is a hormone. Their daughters were exposed in utero, were born, grew up, and in about their twenties developed a very unusual vaginal cancer. In class she called it a very weird and scary phenomenon, seen about twenty years after the women were given D E S. The harm appeared decades later and in a different person from the one who took the drug, which is why it was hard to connect. This is her example of a delayed effect: fast-absorbing compounds are likely to give immediate effects, but some compounds have a very delayed one. The mothers getting the cancer is wrong; they had only nausea. The daughters taking D E S themselves is wrong; their exposure was in utero. A cancer within days is wrong; that would have been easy to link.",
"L13-109": "The workers do not realize they are being exposed because benzene is a gas they cannot see or smell. In class she said that when a little benzene leaks in a workplace, you cannot smell it and cannot see it, and people walking in just get a little headache or feel sleepy. Nothing else warns them. If the leak is never fixed, the same headache and sleepiness return every day, because each day is a new exposure, and no tolerance develops. With chronic low-dose exposure, benzene is a carcinogen that causes leukemias. Tolerance is wrong, because she stressed that the worker still gets the headache and sleepiness every time. The claim that a single exposure produces no symptoms is wrong, because one walk-in does give a headache or sleepiness. The claim that it enters through the skin is wrong, because benzene in her example is a gas that is breathed in.",
"L13-110": "Compound A ends up in the toxic range because its long half-life lets the doses build up. Half-life is the time for half of a compound to be cleared from the blood. A has the longest half-life of the three, about a year on the caption, so very little is cleared between doses. In the repeated-dose panel, each dose is one quarter of the single dose. In class she said it takes a while even for compound A to build up in the system high enough for a toxic response, and that by the third dose A starts causing a toxic response. The claim that each quarter dose of A is above the toxic level is wrong, because one quarter dose does not reach the toxic zone; the build-up does. Saying A has a shorter half-life than C is reversed; A has the longest and C the shortest. Bioactivation to a toxic metabolite is wrong, because the figure is about elimination rate and dosing frequency, and no metabolite is shown.",
"L13-111": "B reaches the toxic range later than A because B is cleared faster than A between doses. All three compounds get the same quarter doses at the same interval, and the toxic concentration is the same line for all of them. The difference is elimination rate. A, with very slow elimination and a half-life of about a year on the caption, is toxic by about the third dose. B, with a half-life about equal to the dosing frequency, about one day, loses roughly half between doses, so it builds up more slowly. In class she said that for B, maybe at the fourth dose you are in the toxic zone, and at the fifth for sure. C, cleared faster than the dosing frequency, never gets there. Saying B is cleared more slowly than A is reversed. Smaller doses of B is wrong, because the doses are the same. A higher toxic concentration for B is wrong, because the toxic line is the same for all three compounds.",
"L13-112": "If the dosing frequency of a drug like compound A were stretched out, more would be cleared between doses, so the peaks would stay lower. Whether repeated doses accumulate depends on the half-life compared with the time between doses. A longer gap gives the body more time to clear each dose, so less remains for the next dose to add onto, and the concentration stops climbing into the toxic zone. In class she said that if this were a medication, the target would be a concentration below toxicity, and the dosing frequencies would be stretched out to end up with something like C, high enough for a therapeutic effect but not so high as to be toxic. Higher peaks with each dose is wrong, because a longer interval lowers the build-up. A shorter half-life is wrong, because changing the interval does not change the drug's half-life; it changes how much is left when the next dose arrives. Bioactivation is wrong, because the dosing interval does not create a toxic metabolite.",
"L13-115": "An older adult may be advised to stay under four grams because their phase two enzymes may be fewer in number. A normal acetaminophen dose is detoxified by two families of phase two enzymes, the glucuronosyl transferases and the sulfotransferases, into a glucuronide or a sulfate. In class she said she suggests to her older parents that four grams in a twenty four hour period is too much, because their phase two enzymes, which she called G S T and S T for short, may be depleting in numbers, so the whole dose may not be handled by them. The excess goes through cytochrome P four fifty two E one to N A P Q I, which is toxic to the liver. Fewer two E one enzymes is wrong, because that would mean less N A P Q I, not more. The claim that phase two enzymes make N A P Q I is wrong; they make the glucuronide and the sulfate. Faster phase two enzymes with age is wrong; that would clear more of the dose safely, the reverse of her concern. Four grams in twenty four hours is the labeled maximum from the Food and Drug Administration.",
"L13-116": "The compound must be named because lethal doses vary enormously between compounds. The L D fifty is the dose that kills half of the animals given a compound, and the lower it is, the less it takes to kill. On her rat table, the L D fifty is ten thousand milligrams per kilogram for ethyl alcohol and one hundred-thousandth of a milligram per kilogram for botulinum toxin. In class she called the range of L D fifty values massive, and said the dose makes the poison, but you have to know what compound you are talking about too. Ethyl alcohol gives a little more grace, while sometimes only a tiny bit of something is very harmful. The saying is attributed to Paracelsus. The claim that compounds have similar L D fifty values is wrong; the range is massive. The claim that route, not dose, decides harm is wrong; route is one factor, but dose remains its own principle. The claim that dose matters only for medications is wrong; the table lists medications, nutrients and true poisons alike.",
"L13-117": "The two curves are there because the chemical acts on more than one target. One organophosphate was given to rodents in graded dosing groups, and two responses were measured at each dose: inhibition of carboxylesterase and inhibition of cholinesterase. Each measured response gets its own curve. In class she said that by using data this way, if the right things are measured, you can see that many chemicals have more than one effect because they have multiple targets, and that these organophosphate exposures inhibit two different kinds of enzymes. She added that this could not be seen without graphing it as an individual dose-response curve. Two different organophosphates is wrong; one compound was given. One curve being a population curve is wrong; both are individual dose-response data, enzyme inhibition at each dose, while a population curve groups deaths. The second curve showing cumulative dose is wrong; cumulative dose was the lesson of the two A A F hamster study, not of this plot.",
"L13-118": "Panel B puts the dose on a logarithmic scale so the data fall on straight lines. On an individual dose-response plot, the y axis is the measured response, here percent enzyme inhibition, and the x axis is the dose each group received. In class she said that sometimes people turn the dose into a logarithmic scale so you get linear lines. Only the x axis changes. The doses, the responses and the two enzyme targets, carboxylesterase and cholinesterase, stay the same. Showing cumulative mortality is wrong, because cumulative mortality is a population dose-response plot, with deaths on the y axis. Probit units are wrong; they belong to the population figure, and she set them aside, saying nobody ever uses them. Reading an L D fifty is wrong, because the L D fifty comes from a population mortality curve, not from enzyme inhibition in individuals.",
"L13-119": "The L D fifty is read from a population curve because it is a dose at which half of a group dies. A population dose-response describes the response of a population to various doses. In class she said you are not looking at individuals getting different doses; you group the responses, such as deaths, and then look at what dose they got. The L D fifty, the dose that kills fifty percent of the animals, is read from the cumulative mortality curve, about one hundred milligrams per kilogram in her figure. Percent enzyme inhibition is wrong, because that is the y axis of the individual dose-response plot. One animal's graded response to dose is wrong, because that is the individual plot; an L D fifty needs the share of a group that died at each dose. The dose where the first deaths appear is wrong, because the dose where an effect first appears is the threshold dose, the other value read from a population curve, about ten milligrams per kilogram in that figure.",
"L13-120": "The response is bad at the lowest doses because, with too little iron, the blood cannot carry oxygen. In class she said that if you do not have enough iron, your blood cannot carry oxygen, and you could suffer the adverse response of death. That is the deficiency arm of the U-shaped curve for an essential substance such as iron, vitamin A or vitamin D. As the dose rises, the person comes out of deficiency into the region of homeostasis, where the blood has the iron it needs. Too much iron causes poisoning, an iron overdose that can also kill. So the curve goes from bad to good to bad. A hormetic effect is wrong, because hormesis is a protective low-dose effect of something non-essential, such as red wine, with no region of homeostasis. A carcinogen with no threshold is wrong, because the no-threshold line is the theory for carcinogens, not the reason for iron's left arm. Bioactivation is wrong, because the left arm is deficiency, not metabolism.",
"L13-121": "Carcinogens have been modeled with a straight line from zero because one molecule might damage D N A and start a cancer. In class she said that on this linear line, the first molecule of what you are exposed to could cause problems. That is a theory for carcinogens: maybe it takes just one molecule, damaging your D N A and kicking you off into cancer. She qualified it with possibly, and said this is what was thought about carcinogens for the longest time. This contrasts with the S-shaped curve of medications and most poisons, which stays flat until a threshold dose. Essential nutrients are wrong; they give a U-shaped curve with a region of homeostasis. Very high L D fifty values are wrong; the L D fifty is a lethality measure from population curves, not the reason for the linear model. Acting right after a single dose is wrong; cancer is a delayed effect, as with D E S and chronic benzene, and the linear model rests on the single-molecule idea.",
"L14-102": "The narrow-index drug is more likely to cause toxicity because its toxic dose is not much higher than its therapeutic dose. The therapeutic index is the toxic dose over the therapeutic dose. A wide index means less chance of toxicity. A narrow index means the toxic dose sits close to the therapeutic dose. In class she said that drugs with a narrow therapeutic index have a toxic dose not too much higher than the therapeutic dose, and that the therapeutic index tells how much error you can get in your dosing. So the same dosing error that a wide-index drug tolerates can push a narrow-index drug into its toxic range. A toxic dose far above the therapeutic dose describes the wide index. The toxic dose in one percent over the effective dose in ninety-nine percent is the margin of safety, while the therapeutic index uses the same percentage, usually fifty, on top and bottom. Slope information comes from the margin of safety, not the therapeutic index.",
"L14-104": "The drug is not clean because some part of the molecule fits other receptors that were not foreseen when it was designed. Off-target means the drug finds receptors it was not intended to find, in the intended tissue or elsewhere, and she calls such a drug unclean. In class she said that maybe when the drug was designed, nobody knew that some molecular aspect of the medication would interact with other receptors, the off-target receptors. Once a drug class shows many side effects while still being therapeutically helpful, chemists go back to the molecule, find the part hitting a different receptor, and change it so the next drug avoids the off-target receptors. Too high a dose at the intended receptor gives an on-target adverse effect, not binding to other receptors. Reaching the intended receptor in the wrong tissue is still called on-target. Down-regulation changes receptor number; it does not make a drug bind other receptors.",
"L14-105": "Grapefruit slows the clearance of terfenadine, so its blood level rises. Terfenadine, brand name Seldane, treated allergies by antagonizing peripheral H one receptors, but it also inhibited cardiac potassium channels, an off-target effect that caused cardiac problems. It is no longer on the market. She took it for allergies in high school and had a racing heart in class, mostly on the mornings her family ate grapefruit. In class she said she was having a drug interaction between the grapefruit and the Seldane. She was not metabolizing and clearing it, so its level in her blood rose and it blocked even more of her potassium channels. A change in the biotransformation and excretion of one compound is a mechanism of chemical interaction. Enzyme induction would lower the drug level, the opposite of what happened. Crossing the blood-brain barrier explains diphenhydramine's drowsiness, not a racing heart. Peripheral H one block is the desired effect, not the cause of the heart symptoms.",
"L14-106": "The newer antihistamines were changed so they do not cross the blood-brain barrier. Diphenhydramine, brand name Benadryl, prevents allergic symptoms by hitting histamine receptors in the periphery. It causes drowsiness by hitting the same histamine receptors in the brain, an on-target adverse effect in the wrong tissue. In class she said that when newer antihistamines were developed, the molecules were tweaked so that they do not cross the blood-brain barrier and cannot get to the histamine receptors in the brain. Cetirizine and fexofenadine therefore still reach the peripheral receptors and prevent allergy symptoms, without the drowsiness. They still act at histamine receptors, so a different receptor is not the reason. They do still reach the peripheral receptors, since that is where they work. Dose is not the reason either, because the drowsiness is a wrong-tissue effect, not a dose that is too high.",
"L14-107": "Thalidomide still caused birth defects because it was taken as a racemic mixture that included the S enantiomer. Thalidomide is racemic, with an R enantiomer and an S enantiomer. The R enantiomer helped with nausea in pregnancy. The S enantiomer is a very potent teratogen. People did not take only the R form; they took both. In class she said it was not just the R enantiomer that people were taking, it was the R and the S, and the S enantiomer was a very potent teratogen. This is the off-target example of an enantiomer hitting an unintended receptor. Thalidomide is used today in oncology. No conversion of the R form inside the fetus was described. Too much dose at the R form's own receptor would be on-target, not the cause. The R form reaching its receptor in the fetus would be on-target in the wrong tissue, which is not how thalidomide is classed.",
"L14-108": "An adverse effect at the intended receptor in the intended tissue can come from too high a dose at the receptor, from chronic activation or inhibition of other effects, or from a deliberate or accidental dosing error. This is an on-target adverse effect. In class she said that when a drug is on target, the reasons for adverse effects are that maybe the dose is too high, or maybe there is chronic activation or inhibition of other effects going on. Keeping the receptor switched on or off for a long time can block something else the body needs. In the right tissue, she called too much dose the usual cause. Too much dose can come from a provider's accidental dosing error, a deliberate overdose, slowed clearance, or a change in receptor number. The drug hitting an unintended receptor subtype, as propranolol does at beta two receptors, is off-target. An S enantiomer in the mixture hitting another receptor, as with thalidomide, is off-target too.",
"L14-109": "Estrogen can be higher along the shorelines of big cities because many young women live there and may be taking oral contraceptives. Most drugs in water are found at about parts per trillion, rarely higher. Estrogen is an exception in some places, depending on the population near where the water is sampled. In class she named Miami, Houston, San Diego and San Francisco, said a lot of young people live there and a lot of estrogen comes out of the wastewater, perhaps because many women are taking oral contraceptives. Where estrogen is higher, populations of frogs, some fish and alligators shift toward female. Livestock feedlots are a source of drugs in water, but not the reason for estrogen on big-city shorelines. Treatment plants fail to remove all drugs; they do not add estrogen. Frogs and alligators are affected by the estrogen, not its source.",
"L14-110": "Frogs are a sentinel, or red-flag, species because they absorb a lot through their skin and are very sensitive. Animals that live in or near water are exposed constantly: by drinking, by breathing through gills in fish, across the skin, and by eating other animals from the water. Frogs take up a lot across their skin, and their high sensitivity means their populations change where drug levels are raised, a warning that things are going wrong. In class she said frogs absorb a lot and are one of our sentinel species, our red flag species, and agreed that frogs can be very sensitive. Where estrogen is higher, frog populations shift toward female. Frogs are not used in drug development; rodents, guinea pigs, dogs and pigs are. Their exposure is not lower than that of humans, since animals living in water are exposed constantly. Being hard to catch and study describes orca whales, not frogs.",
"L14-111": "The evidence is that some species show effects, while others are too hard to catch and study to know. Frogs, some fish and alligators shift from about half male and half female to more female where estrogen levels are higher. For other wildlife, such as orca whales, we do not know. In class she said both answers, yes it is a problem and we do not know, are right. There is some evidence showing a problem, but other wildlife are really hard to capture, and there is not the time, money or ability to study them all. Her key adds that we do not always know which system or organ is affected in wildlife, that data may not have been measured in the correct species or for the correct endpoint, and that we do not know how multiple exposures act. The species used in drug development are not the wildlife in question. Saying no species has shown effects ignores the frogs, fish and alligators. She did not divide the evidence by treated and untreated water, and treated water still carries drugs.",
"L14-112": "Risk assessors would re-evaluate if drug concentrations in drinking water start rising. The answer that humans are probably OK rests on dose. At parts per trillion, even a well-hydrated person does not reach a normal therapeutic daily dose. Sensitive populations, the fetus in utero, children, the elderly and people with diseases, should still not be ignored. In class she said we should be concerned if concentrations of these drugs start rising higher in our drinking waters, and that is probably when risk assessors would say we need to re-evaluate and look again at whether people are having a problem. Another news report or podcast does not change the dose; it only worries the patient. Drinking more water still leaves the dose far below therapeutic. The shift toward female in frogs and alligators is already known and concerns wildlife, so it does not change the answer for humans.",
"L14-113": "Flushing leftover opioids is not improper disposal because flushing is allowed for opioids, as they are a dangerous drug. Under the source principle, people take drugs and excrete the parent drug and metabolites, and some people improperly dispose of medications by flushing them. Her first example was leftover opioids dumped down the toilet, and she withdrew it. In class she said that is allowed because it is a dangerous drug, called it a bad example, and switched to unused antibiotics dumped down the toilet. Flushed antibiotics are the improper disposal example, and they feed the concern about resistant bacteria. Treatment plants do not remove opioids or other drugs completely before release. A flushed drug does not simply break down in toilet water before reaching streams, since current treatment does not get all drugs out. Excretion of metabolites is a separate source, not the reason flushing opioids is acceptable.",
"L14-114": "Bacteria in lake and stream sediments are becoming resistant because they live amid antibiotics that reach the environment through improper disposal. Many microbes survive in the sediments below lakes and streams and in beach sand. As antibiotics are improperly disposed of and get out into that environment, the bacteria living there survive them and become resistant. A person who gets a cut, for example at the beach, may then end up with an infection that cannot be treated. In class she said that as we improperly dispose of our antibiotics, we are creating microbes resistant to them because they are living in that environment. She called this a really big concern and a real problem, separate from the effects on wildlife. Treatment plants fail to remove drugs; they do not add antibiotics. Estrogen was the example of an effect on frogs, fish and alligators, not on bacteria. Warming water came up only for the shift toward female animals.",
"L14-115": "The liver may not recover because regeneration often repairs mild injury but not severe chronic injury. A tissue's ability to regenerate determines whether its injury is reversible. The liver is unique: it can heal and regenerate itself, so many liver injuries are reversible. But she described a big range. In class she said that sometimes the liver can regenerate, that a mild injury can often regenerate, but more severe chronic injury may not, and to be gentle with your livers. Years of heavy drinking give a severe chronic injury rather than a mild one. Ethanol is also her familiar cause of liver fibrosis, called cirrhosis. Liver cells unable to divide describes nerve cells, which is the reason nerve injury is irreversible. Local versus systemic is a different general concept and does not set reversibility. Tolerance is a reduced response to a toxicant, not a block on tissue repair.",
"L14-116": "A birth defect is irreversible because a body part that formed wrongly in the developing fetus stays malformed. Reversibility depends on whether a tissue can repair or regenerate. As a fetus develops, an exposure can disrupt how the limbs or other parts of the body form. In class she said that birth defects are irreversible because an exposure that messes up the way the limbs or other body parts form cannot be fixed. Each structure forms during its own window of gestation, which is why the hamster retinoid study gave different defects on different gestation days. Cancer is likewise irreversible, because cancerous cells are killed or removed, never returned to normal. Mutations passed on in germ cells define a mutagen, while a teratogen acts on the developing fetus through the mother's exposure. Liver regeneration in the mother has nothing to do with it. Delay is a separate concept; diethylstilbestrol is the delayed example, and timing does not decide reversibility.",
"L14-118": "Both are dispositional tolerance because both lower the amount of toxicant reaching the site where the toxic effect is produced. Dispositional tolerance is based on A D M E, meaning absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion. Carbon tetrachloride is metabolized to something even nastier, but with repeated exposure it inhibits its own metabolism, so less of the toxic metabolite forms. Cadmium induces the metal-carrying protein, which she called a metal carrying enzyme, that helps the kidneys eliminate it, so more is cleared. In class she said these tolerances are based on A D M E, the dispositional tolerances. The other mechanism is reduced responsiveness, which is pharmacodynamic: the target, such as a receptor population, responds less. A target tissue responding less to the same concentration is reduced responsiveness. Down-regulating receptors is the reduced responsiveness example. Inducing their own metabolism is wrong for carbon tetrachloride, which inhibits it.",
"L14-119": "An agent that causes mutations in germ cells is a mutagen because germ cell changes can be passed down to offspring. Genotoxicity is damage to D N A in general, and it can occur in somatic cells, the ordinary body cells, as well as in germ cells. Mutagenicity refers to a genetic alteration that is transmissible, one that can be passed down to offspring, and that transmission happens through germ cells. In class she said mutagenicity refers to the fact that it is transmissible, that you can pass it down to your offspring, and that if mutations happen only in your germ cells, it is a mutagen. Cancer in the parent is one possible result of genotoxicity, along with birth defects and genetically based disorders, not the definition of a mutagen. Somatic changes are not passed down, so the reversed statement has it backwards. A mutagen is not tied to one organ such as the gonads, since genotoxicity is non-organ specific.",
"L15-102": "The worse outcome is explained by poorer clearance of the deposited particles. Where particles land depends on their size and shape and on the breathing pattern. What stays depends on clearance. Coughing and the cilia of the upper respiratory tract move deposited material up and out of the lungs, and particle clearance is one of the listed reasons the lungs are susceptible. In class she said that if that clearance is working, it helps you, but some patients don't have a good clearance mechanism, so they have a worse outcome than others. A heavier breathing pattern does increase deposition, but the two workers worked at the same rate. Water solubility sets how far a gas travels, and both breathed the same dust. Oxidative burden applies to every lung breathing the same air, so it does not separate two workers with the same exposure.",
"L15-103": "Both kinds of immune cells add to injury because their activity releases reactive oxygen species. In the lung, white blood cells drawn in to fight a pathogen or clean up tissue damage release reactive oxygen species, which adds to the oxidative burden. In the liver, Kupffer cells chewing up dead tissue release inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species, which add to the inflammation. In class she said the activity of those white blood cells will also release reactive oxygen species, and that the Kupffer cells, as they chew up dead tissue, release inflammatory compounds and reactive oxygen species, adding to the risk to the liver. Bioactivation to toxic metabolites is done by metabolizing enzymes such as cytochrome P four fifty two E one in hepatocytes, not by immune cells. Falling oxygen along the liver acinus comes from hepatocytes using it, not from immune cells. Taking up and concentrating a toxin is a reason the liver and kidney are susceptible, not the immune-cell reason.",
"L15-104": "The difference is that brain capillaries have tight junctions and are wrapped in tightly joined glial cells. A systemic capillary has fenestrations and loosely joined pericytes, so solutes leave it easily. In a brain capillary the vessel cells touch tightly, and the glial cells outside are tightly connected, so solutes cannot pass between cells. A compound must go through the cells, which takes lipophilicity and small size. In class she said this tightness creates the blood-brain barrier and protects us especially from non-lipophilic or larger compounds, which are too large and don't have the chemistry to get through the vascular walls and the glial cells. Larger fenestrations are wrong. Fenestrations belong to systemic capillaries. Schwann cells wrap peripheral axons to make myelin, not blood vessels. Loosely joined pericytes surround systemic capillaries, which is why the compound leaves them easily. The barrier is still developing at birth, so in a young child such a compound might get in.",
"L15-105": "An exposure that lowers oxygen and energy causes trouble in the nervous system because neurons depend on aerobic metabolism. The nervous system has very high energy requirements. Its cells are often very long and do a great deal of work, including moving nutrients and waste along their axons. They make that energy by aerobic metabolism, so a fall in oxygen cuts the energy their work depends on. In class she said the nervous system has very, very high energy requirements and depends on aerobic metabolism, so if any exposure affects the oxygen and energy available to those cells, you are going to have a problem. Neurons holding glucose reserves for use without oxygen is the reverse of her point. Myelin speeds conduction, and she did not describe it as limiting oxygen. The oxygen gradient that leaves blood hypoxic belongs to zone three of the liver acinus, not the brain. The nervous system's problem is its energy demand.",
"L15-106": "Long peripheral nerves need so much energy because they transport nutrients and waste along their axons. These cells run from the spinal cord all the way down the limbs. Each must produce a very large cellular volume and move products down, and up, the axon. That axonal transport adds to the very high energy requirement that makes the nervous system susceptible to neurotoxins. In class she said those nerves are long, the cells must produce a lot of volume to stay healthy, and they need energy to transport waste and nutrients up and down those axons. They do not make myelin. Myelin is made by the myelinating cells, oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system and Schwann cells in peripheral nerves. Neurons depend on aerobic metabolism, not anaerobic glycolysis. And rapid replacement of cell bodies was not her reason.",
"L15-107": "If a toxin damaged the Schwann cells, conduction along the nerve would slow. Schwann cells wrap around the axon again and again, building layer upon layer of lipid membrane, the myelin sheath. Myelin lets electrical impulses move down the nerve quickly, and it is found in no other organ. In class she said the Schwann cells help electric impulses move down the nerve cells quickly, and if the myelin sheath is damaged, neurotransmission is slower, with symptoms such as tingling of the hands and feet. Metronidazole, from her own experience, is her example of this myelinopathy. Conduction would not speed up. Losing myelin slows it. Death of the entire neuron with gliosis is neuronopathy, a different pattern. A cell that looks normal under the microscope describes interruption of neurotransmission, while damaged myelin is visible.",
"L15-108": "Myelinopathy may not be the worst type because the axon can be remyelinated, though slowly. In myelinopathy the toxin damages the myelin or the myelinating cells, while the axon and the cell body survive. Myelinating cells can divide and cover the bare axon again, so the damage can be repaired over time, unlike the death of a whole neuron. Remyelination is much less effective in the central nervous system than in the peripheral nervous system. In class she said you can get remyelination of the axons, but it takes quite a while, so myelinopathy may not be the worst type of neurotoxicity. Myelin damage does produce symptoms, slowed conduction with tingling of the hands and feet. A neuron that dies is neuronopathy, and in myelinopathy the neuron survives. Disturbing the synapse is the neurotransmission pattern, while myelinopathy damages the sheath along the axon.",
"L15-109": "The compound can cause symptoms because the nervous system depends on neurotransmission to do its work. A neuron releases a neurotransmitter, which crosses the synaptic cleft and binds the postsynaptic cell, and the transmitter is then taken back up. Disturbing release, binding or reuptake changes that signaling, so function fails even though no cell is damaged and the cell looks normal. In class she said that if anything disturbs the reuptake of those neurotransmitters, you can end up with symptoms, because you are messing with the usual neurotransmission. Organophosphates are her example of this pattern. Reuptake does not build myelin. Schwann cells and oligodendrocytes make it. Axonopathy is structural damage to the axon, and here no cell is damaged. Starving neurons of oxygen and energy is a separate reason for susceptibility, while this compound acts at the synapse.",
"L15-111": "The liver is most likely to be affected because it does most of the body's drug metabolism. A rare variant that changes how a drug is metabolized shows up where the metabolism happens. That person may turn the drug into a hepatotoxic metabolite that most people do not make. Reactions like this were once called idiosyncratic and unexplained, and pharmacogenomics is now explaining some of them. In class she said that because the liver does most of the metabolism of drugs and other compounds, a person with an unusual genetic variation will probably have the liver affected, not the kidneys so much and not the lungs, because the liver is such a workhorse in metabolism. Size is the trap. She said being a large organ has nothing to do with it. Sensitivity to vasoconstrictors is the kidney's unique feature. Kupffer cells are a real reason for liver susceptibility, but they do not explain a reaction caused by a variant in metabolism.",
"L15-113": "The kidney is exposed because much of its large blood flow has just passed through the liver. The kidneys receive a large delivery of blood, and that blood carries the metabolites the liver has just made, toxic ones included. The kidney cells then take up, concentrate and metabolize those compounds, so the kidney is often injured by what the liver makes. In class she said the kidney gets lots of blood flowing to it, especially blood that has gone through the liver, with metabolites created by the liver heading to the kidney. Bile does not carry the metabolite to the kidney. Bile drains into the intestine. Kupffer cells belong to the liver, not the kidney. Constriction of the afferent and efferent arterioles lowers renal blood flow and the glomerular filtration rate. It does not trap a metabolite.",
"L15-114": "Concentrating urine raises the kidney's risk because it also concentrates the poison, which drives more of it into the tubular cells. The processes that concentrate urine also concentrate potential poisons in the renal tubule, enhancing their transport into the tubular cells. Like liver cells, kidney cells take compounds up and metabolize them, so toxicity right in the kidney itself becomes likely. In class she said the cells in the kidney take compounds up into themselves, concentrating solutes in the cells. Diverting blood away from the glomerulus is wrong. Reduced glomerular flow comes from vasoconstriction of the arterioles, a separate reason. Diluting the poison is the opposite of what concentrating does. Removing oxygen from the tubular cells is wrong too. The oxygen gradient is the liver's zone story.",
"L15-115": "Crystalluria and tubular toxicity are explained by the chemistry of the drugs themselves. For most site-specific kidney injury she singled out metabolism. Which enzymes are expressed in which part of the kidney decides where a toxic metabolite forms. These two injuries are different. They come from the drug's own chemical properties, not from a local enzyme making a toxic metabolite. Classic examples are sulfonamides, methotrexate and acyclovir for crystalluria, and aminoglycosides and cisplatin for tubular toxicity. In class she said the tubular toxicity and the crystalluria have to do more with the chemistry of those drugs. Where cytochrome P four fifty two E one is expressed explains acetaminophen injury in zone three of the liver, not these. An allergic response appearing in the kidney was her proton pump inhibitor story, interstitial nephritis. Constriction of the afferent and efferent arterioles is the vasoconstriction category.",
"L15-116": "The reaction could go unnoticed because the allergy appeared as kidney injury, not as hives. Proton pump inhibitors can cause interstitial nephritis, especially in patients with an aspirin allergy, as an allergic response to the drug. That response does not show itself as hives, trouble breathing or a rash. It shows up as kidney damage, so only the blood work, with a high serum creatinine, caught it. In her father's case it was found because his physical was rescheduled, so blood work came after a few months on the drug, and by then he needed erythropoietin for a while. In class she said the allergic response is manifesting as interstitial nephritis, so it's not like an allergic response where you get hives or an inability to breathe. Crystals blocking the tubule is wrong. These drugs belong under interstitial nephritis, not crystalluria. Serum creatinine does not fall with kidney injury. It rises. And the injury happened while he was taking the drug, not after stopping it.",
"L15-120": "Toxicokinetic-based treatments are carried out to decrease the amount of poison in the body, hoping to reduce the potential for end-organ damage. The four methods all work on what the body does with the poison. Prevent absorption, so less gets in. Inhibit toxication, so less is bioactivated to a toxic form. Enhance metabolism, so more is detoxified. Enhance elimination, so more gets out. In class she listed these four and asked the room to see how all four are kinetically based treatments. Blocking the receptor on which the poison acts is a receptor antagonist, a pharmacologically based treatment. Binding the poison in the blood with a chelator is inactivation of the poison, the second of the three approaches. Giving a drug with the opposite pharmacologic action is the definition of pharmacological treatment.",
"L15-121": "Whole bowel irrigation is not used with an ileus or a blockage because the solution has to pass through an open gut. Whole bowel irrigation is what a colonoscopy preparation does. The patient drinks a solution such as polyethylene glycol, and it flushes the gut contents, and the poison, through and out. A blockage stops that flow, so the flush cannot carry the poison out. In class she said people must have no ileus and no blockages in the gut, and their system must be open so that all of it can come through. She added that it is messy and nursing staff do not like doing it, but it is useful. The solution is not meant to be absorbed and carry the poison. It is meant to pass through. Binding the poison is what activated charcoal does. Vomiting and aspiration are the risk of emesis and of charcoal.",
"L15-123": "Repeated doses of activated charcoal can remove some absorbed drug because drug secreted into the bile re-enters the gut. In enterohepatic recirculation, a drug goes to the liver, is moved into the bile, is put back into the small intestine, and then returns to the liver. Each pass brings absorbed drug back into the gut, where a later dose of charcoal can bind it, and the bound drug leaves in the feces. This works only for a compound that both recirculates and sticks to charcoal. In class she said a second dose of charcoal might catch some of the compound as it is re-injected into the small intestine from the liver through the bile. Charcoal is not absorbed into the blood. It stays in the gut. Charcoal does not speed liver metabolism. Enhancing metabolism is a different toxicokinetic method. Pulling drug out of fat and muscle is the large volume of distribution problem with hemodialysis.",
"L16-005": "Both pathways start with the same enzyme, alcohol dehydrogenase. On the methanol path, alcohol dehydrogenase turns methanol into formaldehyde, and formaldehyde dehydrogenase turns formaldehyde into formic acid. Formic acid is the toxic product; it kills the receptors at the back of the retina, so sight is lost. On the ethylene glycol path, alcohol dehydrogenase turns ethylene glycol into glycoaldehyde, aldehyde dehydrogenase makes glycolic acid, and several more steps give oxalic acid. Oxalic acid precipitates as microcrystals in small vessels, and renal toxicity is common. The trap is mixing the two paths: formaldehyde and formic acid belong to methanol, and glycoaldehyde, glycolic acid and oxalic acid belong to ethylene glycol. The shared first enzyme is why one antidote, ethanol or fomepizole, serves both.",
"L16-013": "Each toxidrome pairs with its example drugs and signs. Sympathomimetic: cocaine and amphetamine, with agitation, tremor and diaphoresis. Anticholinergic: diphenhydramine and atropine, with delirium, ileus and flushing. Cholinergic: organophosphates, with somnolence or coma, SLUDGE and fasciculation. SLUDGE stands for the muscarinic effects salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, gastric cramping and emesis. Opioid: heroin and oxycodone, with somnolence or coma; her key adds decreased heart and breath rates and decreased pupil size, so enlarged pupils are not part of it. Salicylate: aspirin, with confusion, diaphoresis and vomiting. The table also lists the sedative hypnotics, benzodiazepines and barbiturates, with somnolence or coma, and the calcium channel blocker verapamil. The trap is the two acetylcholine patterns: anticholinergic brings delirium, ileus and flushing, while cholinergic brings SLUDGE and fasciculation.",
"L16-033": "Each chelating agent pairs with its metals. Edetate disodium chelates lead. Dimercaprol, also called British anti-Lewisite, chelates gold, arsenic, lead and mercury. Succimer chelates lead, cadmium, mercury and arsenic. Deferoxamine chelates iron; her key says it chelates the circulating ferric iron. Deferasirox also chelates iron, and it is the oral one: orally bioavailable and recently approved by the Food and Drug Administration. Penicillamine and trientine chelate copper, usually for patients with Wilson's disease, a genetically based disease with very high copper levels. In class she said the table holds the most common chelating agents and which metal each one grabs. The traps are the overlaps. Lead appears for edetate disodium, dimercaprol and succimer. Gold appears only for dimercaprol, and cadmium only for succimer. Iron belongs to deferoxamine and deferasirox, and copper to penicillamine and trientine.",
"QZ3-001": "An off-target adverse effect happens when a drug interacts with a receptor that is not its therapeutic target. In class she said off-target is when the drug is finding other receptors we did not intend it to find, either in the correct tissue or in the incorrect tissue. She gave three off-target examples. Terfenadine blocked peripheral H one receptors as intended, but caused arrhythmias by inhibiting the cardiac potassium channel. In racemic thalidomide, the R enantiomer is the anti-nausea agent and the S enantiomer is a potent teratogen. Propranolol slows the heart through beta one receptors, but can worsen asthma by blocking beta two receptors in airway smooth muscle. The trap is the intended receptor in the wrong location. That is still called on-target, as with sleepiness from diphenhydramine. She said she does not like that vocabulary, but it is classically called on-target. A dose at the intended receptor that is too high is also on-target, from deliberate or accidental dosing errors, changes in pharmacokinetics, or a change in receptor number. Giving the wrong drug defines neither category, because the two categories describe which receptor the drug hits.",
"QZ3-002": "Fibrosis is the non-organ-specific endpoint. In fibrosis, a compound causes the body to lay down collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins, and the tissue becomes stiff. In class she said that whether fibrosis is in the lungs or the liver, if you look at the cells and the chemistry of the fibrotic tissue, it is the same, so the same pathology creates it. Pulmonary fibrosis does not look one way and liver fibrosis another. Her four non-organ-specific endpoints are fibrosis, genotoxicity, cancer and teratogenesis. Nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity and hepatotoxicity are each defined by the one organ or system they damage: the kidney, the nervous system and the liver. With the lungs, they are her four organ-specific examples. The trap is that particular compounds target particular organs. Amiodarone, bleomycin and paraquat cause lung fibrosis, amiodarone can also cause liver fibrosis, and ethanol causes liver fibrosis, called cirrhosis. Fibrosis is still non-organ specific, because its pathology is the same in every tissue.",
"QZ3-003": "Cancer is non-organ specific because cancers in different organs may be caused by the same pathological mechanism. In class she agreed that the likelihood of cancer differs from one organ to another, and said that is a true statement. But looking at the cellular nature of different tumors has shown that some lung cancers are caused by the same genetic mutation seen in colon cancers, and that some lung cancers are what is also seen in some breast cancers. So at the cellular level the pathology is the same, even though one cancer is in the lung and another is in the breast. She said that is not always the case, but it is seen, and that is why cancer is called a non-organ-specific endpoint, along with fibrosis, genotoxicity and teratogenesis. The wrong choices fail for these reasons. The category does not rest on what is unknown about cancer; it rests on the shared cellular pathology that has been found. Calling cancer an organ-specific outcome contradicts her list. Saying the pathology of cancers in different organs has nothing in common is the opposite of her reason.",
"QZ3-004": "Three choices are correct: effects on female and male reproductive potential, risks to the baby of use while breastfeeding, and risks to the baby of use during pregnancy. The Food and Drug Administration used to rank drugs only on whether they cause birth defects, on a scale from yes to no. The Pregnancy and Lactation Labeling Rule of two thousand fourteen kept that ranking of how teratogenic a drug is, and added two kinds of information in the same labeling place. In class she said the labeling now covers the effects a drug might have on fertility, including male fertility, because a drug may affect sperm count or sperm viability. It also says whether a nursing mother should take the drug, because children should not ingest certain medications in breast milk. The wrong choice is how the drug affects a child when it is given to the child directly. That is pediatric dosing, not pregnancy, fertility or lactation information.",
"QZ3-005": "Medium-sized particles are the ones that reach the deep, alveolar regions of the lungs. In class she grouped inhaled particles as large, medium and small, and said the actual size does not matter, only the concept. Medium particles are the most concerning. They are heavy enough not to be breathed out with every breath, and small enough to go around the curves of the bronchioles into the lower parts of the lung. In the still moment between breathing in and breathing out, they fall out of the air and are deposited there. Large particles come in fast, cannot turn the corners, and impact where the upper airways bifurcate. In her grouping, very small particles are so light that they do not get past the upper airway, the nose and upper throat, and are breathed back out. Her key says medium particles; in class she placed them in the lower parts of the lung and did not use the word alveolar. The shape of a particle, as with the long, thin, sharp particles of asbestos, and the breathing pattern, as in jogging compared with walking, also change where particles deposit.",
"QZ3-006": "In neuronopathy, the entire neuron dies. In the figure of patterns of neurotoxic injury, neuron B shows it. In class she asked what the neurotoxin did to that nerve cell and answered that it killed the whole thing. Astrocytes often proliferate in response, giving neuronal loss and gliosis. The other choices are the other patterns. Degeneration of the axon is axonopathy, neuron C: only the axon is damaged, and the cell body and myelinating cells are not affected. Injury to the myelinating cells is myelinopathy, neuron D, where the Schwann cells making the myelin sheath are damaged; remyelination is much less effective in the central nervous system than in the peripheral. She said metronidazole causes myelinopathy, with tingling of the hands and feet. Too much neurotransmitter in the synaptic cleft is a disturbance of neurotransmission, neuron E: too much excitation or too little transmitter released, with no cell death. Organophosphates are her example. She said a neurotoxin usually causes one of these discrete kinds of neurotoxicity.",
"QZ3-007": "False. Nephrotoxins do not all damage the entire kidney; a nephrotoxin often causes one discrete kind of renal injury. In class she said that when the kidneys get damaged, they are often damaged by a compound causing a discrete kind of injury. The site-specific nephrotoxicity table sorts chemically induced acute renal failure into kinds: prerenal, vasoconstriction, crystalluria, tubular toxicity, endothelial injury, glomerulopathy and interstitial nephritis, each with its own example compounds. She said the list of drugs need not be memorized; what matters is these different kinds of renal damage in general. The most important reason for site-specific injury, she said, is the metabolism going on in the renal cells, which depends on what enzymes are expressed in what part. The non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs worry her most; they can cause interstitial nephritis and vasoconstriction. True is the trap, because the word all makes the statement false.",
"QZ3-008": "Two reasons explain site-specific liver injury: an oxygen gradient across the zones, and uneven expression of metabolizing enzymes. Blood comes into zone one relatively oxygenated and leaves zone three by the central vein relatively hypoxic, because the hepatocytes use up the oxygen as it flows past. If the enzymes in zone one need oxygen to do their work, the high oxygen there helps them, and that metabolism may produce a hepatotoxic metabolite. Her key puts it as oxygen highest in zone one and lowest in zone three. Second, metabolizing enzymes are not homogeneously expressed. Many phase two enzymes are in zone one, and cytochrome P four fifty two E one is higher in zone three. That is why an acetaminophen overdose, bioactivated by that enzyme to N A P Q I, kills hepatocytes in zone three. In class she said oxygen gradients and non-homogeneous expression of metabolizing enzymes contribute to where liver damage happens. The wrong pairings reverse her facts. The P four fifties are not expressed evenly, and zone three gets the least oxygen. Kupffer cells go all over the liver, not just one zone, and being a large organ has nothing to do with susceptibility. Zone three, not zone one, is next to the central vein, and many phase two enzymes are in zone one.",
"QZ3-009": "False. A teratogen does not cause only one kind of birth defect; for a given teratogen, the time of exposure during gestation influences the type of defect. Her classic example is a retinoid given to pregnant hamsters. Limb defects were the expected effect. When the mothers were dosed on different gestation days, the offspring also had small eyes, spina bifida, cleft palate, forelimb shortening and tail defects. In class she said this is a classic example of why a teratogen is not necessarily organ specific: depending on the time of the mother's exposure during pregnancy, other effects can happen. That is why teratogenesis is a non-organ-specific endpoint. The reverse also holds. Different compounds can cause the same defect: methanol given on day seven of gestation and T C D D on day eleven both produce cleft palate. True is the trap, because the word all makes the statement false.",
"QZ3-010": "Fomepizole is an example of inhibiting bioactivation, also called inhibiting toxication. Methanol and ethylene glycol are relatively harmless until alcohol dehydrogenase metabolizes them. Methanol becomes formaldehyde and then formic acid, causing metabolic acidosis and blindness. Ethylene glycol becomes oxalic acid, which can form crystals in small vessels and damage organs such as the kidneys. In class she said fomepizole binds to and inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase and prevents the metabolism of methanol or ethylene glycol, and that is the way we block their bioactivation to nasty metabolites. Ethanol does the same by outcompeting the poison for the enzyme. Her table key adds that fomepizole is the stronger inhibitor, with fewer adverse effects than ethanol. She said there are not many cases where bioactivation can be blocked; these two are the classic examples. The wrong choices are the other toxicokinetic methods. Enhancing elimination removes the poison itself, by ion trapping or hemodialysis. Enhancing detoxification gives cofactors to speed a protective reaction, as for cyanide and acetaminophen. Preventing absorption, with activated charcoal for instance, acts before the poison is absorbed.",
"QZ3-011": "H I E T, high-dose insulin, treats an overdose of an extended-release calcium channel blocker. Her table key lists six antidotes for beta-blocker overdose: atropine, calcium, glucagon, H I E T, vasopressors, and intravenous twenty percent fatty acid emulsion. For calcium channel blocker overdose her key gives the same antidotes and the same mechanisms as for beta-blocker overdose. For H I E T, her key says only that the mechanisms of its inotropic effects are not understood, and the Marraffa article says the mechanism has not been clearly delineated. The extended-release form does not change the antidote. It changes how long an asymptomatic patient must be watched: a hospital if found within eighteen hours of a modified-release product, or twenty-four hours for modified-release verapamil, compared with six hours for immediate release. Extended-release products tend to form bezoars that break apart at unpredictable times. The wrong choices belong to other poisons. Ethanol inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase in toxic alcohol poisoning. Glutathione is what N-acetylcysteine replenishes in acetaminophen overdose. Sodium thiosulfate treats cyanide: with the help of the body's own enzyme rhodanese, it binds cyanide so the cyanide can be renally eliminated.",
"QZ3-012": "Glucagon increases intracellular cyclic A M P. Her table key gives its mechanism as increasing cardiac cellular cyclic A M P to increase heart rate. Glucagon is one of the six antidotes listed for beta-blocker overdose, and her key gives calcium channel blocker overdose the same antidotes as beta-blocker overdose, so the mechanism is the same in a calcium channel blocker overdose. Both overdoses cause hypotension and bradycardia, and glucagon counters the slow heart rate. Each wrong choice is the mechanism of another antidote. A cofactor for rhodanese activity points to cyanide, where sodium thiosulfate binds cyanide with the help of the body's own enzyme rhodanese, and the complex is renally eliminated. Conjugating the drug describes an inactivator binding the poison, as digoxin immune Fab binds digoxin; glucagon does not bind the calcium channel blocker. Inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase is ethanol or fomepizole, for methanol and ethylene glycol.",
"QZ3-013": "Pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from its binding site on acetylcholinesterase. An organophosphate bound to acetylcholinesterase inactivates the enzyme, so acetylcholine builds up in the synaptic cleft. In class she said pralidoxime pulls off the organophosphate and puts it onto itself, and when it does that, it can be eliminated. Her key says it removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase. She stressed the word sometimes: some organophosphates undergo aging once bound, when part of their structure falls off, and then pralidoxime no longer works. Whether that happens depends on the organophosphate's chemical structure. Atropine is the trap. It also treats organophosphate poisoning, but as a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist it blocks the excess acetylcholine and leaves the organophosphate bound. Physostigmine raises acetylcholine by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase, and treats belladonna alkaloids such as jimsonweed. Flumazenil antagonizes the GABA receptor in a benzodiazepine overdose.",
"QZ3-014": "Increased pupil size is not part of the opioid toxidrome. Her table key gives opioid overdose as somnolence, coma, decreased heart and breath rates, and decreased pupils. So decreased heart rate, decreased breath rate and somnolence all belong, and the pupils get smaller, not larger. Dilated pupils belong to the belladonna alkaloids; in class she said jimsonweed, a belladonna alkaloid, will make your eyes dilate. The falling heart and breathing rates also separate opioid overdose from benzodiazepine overdose, which shows central nervous system depression with relatively normal vitals. The antidote for opioids is naloxone, a competitive antagonist of opioid receptors. In chronic opioid users it can trigger withdrawal.",
"QZ3-015": "False. Kinetics can change in an overdose. In class she said the kinetics of a drug in an overdose often differ from its kinetics at a normal therapeutic dose, and most often volume of distribution and clearance change significantly. A drug with a large volume of distribution, over five liters per kilogram, is hard to remove from the blood by hemodialysis, because most of it is sequestered in sites such as lipids, muscle and bone. Antidepressants, antipsychotics, antimalarials, opioids, propranolol and verapamil are examples. Slowed tablet dissolution or gastric emptying delays absorption and the peak toxic effects. A drug may injure the gut and increase its own absorption. Saturated liver metabolism reduces the first-pass effect, so more drug reaches the circulation. Protein-binding capacity can be exceeded, raising the free fraction. Elimination can shift from first order to zero order, extending the half-life. True is the trap, because the word never makes the statement false.",
"QZ3-016": "False. Emesis is not the first line for an orally ingested poison; it is no longer used. In class she said the DiPiro chapter states that we no longer use emesis. Syrup of ipecac used to be kept at home to make a child vomit, but it is no longer even sold in the United States, and it is not used in hospitals because it is too risky. The biggest risk of emesis is aspiration. Her guideline key for both beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker ingestions says do not induce emesis, and she said you don't make them vomit at home, because it is too dangerous. By patient outcome data, activated charcoal is probably the best of the methods to prevent absorption. Experienced emergency rooms and toxicologists may still try gastric lavage or whole bowel irrigation for a massive, very dangerous ingestion.",
"QZ3-017": "Less than sixty minutes after ingestion is the keyed time frame. Single-dose activated charcoal can only bind the poison while it is still in the stomach and small intestine, so it has to be given very soon after the ingestion. Her key puts the window at less than sixty minutes. In lecture she said it probably needs to be done within one to two hours, and later said up to two hours: they used to say just an hour, but they have lengthened that a little. Either way, the earliest choice is the best one. Two to four hours is mostly past her window; she said it does not work so well if you wait four to six hours, because stuff moves on through. Any time frame fails for the same reason, and her own answer to whether charcoal can be used any time was up to two hours. No sooner than twenty-four hours is far too late, because the poison has left the gut. By patient outcomes charcoal is probably the best absorption-prevention method, but not everything sticks to it. Multiple-dose charcoal is the separate case for compounds that undergo enterohepatic recirculation.",
"QZ3-018": "The statement that is not correct is that metabolism can be enhanced by inducing metabolizing enzymes. Induction is real, but in class she said it takes a few days, so we can't treat our patient fast enough with that. Instead, metabolism, also called detoxification, is enhanced by giving cofactors for the reactions. Her two examples are cyanide, where sodium thiosulfate binds cyanide with the help of the body's own enzyme rhodanese, and acetaminophen, where N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione. The other statements are correct. Ion trapping enhances renal clearance: sodium bicarbonate traps weakly acidic poisons such as salicylates and phenobarbital in the urine, and it is not done for weakly basic toxins. Bioactivation can be inhibited for methanol and ethylene glycol, with ethanol or fomepizole. Preventing absorption is the first thing tried; she said that if it fails and the poison reaches the circulation, the other approaches are used. Toxicokinetic treatment is done to decrease the amount of poison in the body, hoping to reduce end-organ damage.",
"QZ3-019": "The exception is that the antidote does not need to be cleared once it binds the poison. In class she said the toxin and inactivator complex then has to get cleared from the body. An inactivator binds the toxin and prevents it from interacting with its target tissues. The complex must have low toxicity, and she changed that in class from low to no toxicity: the complex must not have its own activity. The inactivator must have high affinity for the toxin, so that massive doses of it, which could cause side effects of their own, are not needed. Heavy metal chelators, which bind lead, mercury, cadmium, iron and copper, add more requirements. They must bind the metal more tightly than tissue macromolecules, be water soluble, non-toxic and readily cleared, and have low binding affinity for endogenous ions such as calcium. So the other three choices are real requirements, and only the claim that the bound antidote need not be cleared is false.",
"QZ3-020": "Hypersensitivity is the adverse response often seen with variants in H L A genes. Of her four target areas for pharmacogenomics, the newest is the immune system. In class she said that people with certain genetic variations in their H L A antigens are going to respond badly to some medications, and that every year the list of drugs tied to severe adverse reactions in the immune system grows a little longer. When the immune system mounts a response against a drug, she gave two broad categories: hypersensitivity, an allergic response of type one, two, three or four, and autoimmune reactions. The wrong choices belong to her other target areas. Decreased receptor response comes from variants in drug targets such as receptors, for example the beta-adrenergic receptors. Changes in drug transport come from transporter variants such as those of P-glycoprotein, encoded by the A B C B one gene. Decreased metabolism comes from variants in metabolizing enzymes such as the cytochrome P four fifties.",
"QZ3-021": "True. Clinically significant relationships between genotype and drug response exist and should help guide patient care. In class she said MacKinnon and colleagues, in two thousand twenty-three, looked at the records of thousands of patients and found that more than half of the patients evaluated were prescribed medications where pharmacogenomics is important in their use. Pharmacogenomics serves two goals in patient outcomes: to prevent toxicity or to ensure efficacy. A genetic variant can change pharmacokinetics, through a metabolizing enzyme or a transporter, or pharmacodynamics, through a drug target, and either can change the response. She also said pharmacists are among the best educated in pharmacogenomics and have a big role in caring for patients from this angle, although Wondrasek and colleagues found that pharmacists do not feel confident in using it. False is the trap; it contradicts the finding that pharmacogenomics matters for more than half of the patients evaluated.",
"QZ3-022": "A haplotype is more than one genetic variant that group together and co-exist at a higher frequency than would occur by chance. The official definition she gave is a set of alleles at multiple loci, or areas of a gene, that co-exist on the same chromosome. In class she said a haplotype has a statistical significance: it occurs at a frequency greater than random chance. Her figure showed single nucleotide polymorphisms at three loci in a population, travelling together as sets such as A G T, G T A and A G A. When a set of alleles co-exists more often than random chance predicts, that is called linkage disequilibrium. The wrong choices describe single variants. A variant that occurs in at least one percent of a population is a polymorphism. A variant in less than one percent of the population is a rare variant. One variant locus at a higher-than-chance frequency is still only one locus, and a haplotype needs multiple loci.",
"QZ3-023": "Variants in drug metabolizing enzymes are the best characterized. In class she said the first area where pharmacogenomics is used the most, and where we know the most, is metabolizing enzymes, above all the cytochrome P four fifties, and now other phase one and phase two enzymes as well. She called them the low hanging fruit. Patients who could not metabolize and clear certain drugs reached drug blood concentrations that were too high, sometimes fatal, and those reactions were the most apparent and the most dangerous, so they were explained first. Her four target areas came in this order: metabolizing enzymes, then drug transporters such as P-glycoprotein, then drug targets such as receptors or enzymes, then immunological targets such as H L A variants. So transporters came second, not first. Drug targets are more recent still; she said that area has not moved forward as fast as the metabolizing enzymes or the drug transporters, and it plays its biggest role today in oncology.",
"RMK3-005": "Each source is sorted by whether the step that releases the drug can change. Flushing could be reduced by other disposal methods of drugs, because flushing unused medications is improper disposal, a choice. Animal waste could be reduced by controls on feedlots and farms, because livestock there are given hormones and antibiotics. Human waste cannot be reduced, because people take medications to treat and prevent disease, and the parent drug and metabolites leave the body. The trap is giving human waste a fix. The wildlife sources are the same as for humans, since these sources reach the same water.",
"RMK3-015": "All four wildlife exposure entries follow from one fact: aquatic animals live in the contaminated water. The routes are ingestion of drinking water, ingestion of food, which includes other animals living in that water, and dermal, with frogs absorbing a lot. The duration is exposure for their entire life. The exposure is repeated, not single. The frequency is constant if their home range is continually contaminated, and contamination levels may vary even in one location. The trap is the human entries: for humans the routes are drinking water, fish and recreational skin contact, and duration, repetition and frequency depend on how people are exposed and where they live.",
"RMK3-031": "Each reading has its own authors. Rodriguez-Mozaz and Weinberg wrote the meeting report on pharmaceuticals in water. Wax and colleagues of the American Association of Poison Control Centers wrote the beta-blocker ingestion guideline. Olson and colleagues, the same panel, wrote the calcium channel blocker ingestion guideline. Marraffa, Cohen and Howland wrote the primer on antidotes for toxicological emergencies. The trap is the two guidelines: the same twelve-member panel wrote both, and only the first author changes, Wax for beta blockers and Olson for calcium channel blockers.",
"TBL3-023": "Each beta-blocker antidote has its own mechanism. Atropine increases heart rate. Calcium is given to try to drive calcium into cardiac cells. Glucagon increases cardiac cellular cyclic A M P to increase heart rate. H I E T has inotropic effects whose mechanisms are not understood. Vasopressors increase blood pressure. Intravenous twenty percent fatty acid emulsion acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough. The overdose leaves a slow heart and low blood pressure, so most of these raise heart rate or pressure. The trap is atropine and glucagon, which share a goal; glucagon reaches the faster heart rate through cyclic A M P. For calcium channel blocker overdose her key gives the same six antidotes and mechanisms.",
"TBL3-048": "Iron overdose runs on a timeline. Within one to six hours come vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea. Within six to twenty four hours come lethargy, coma, seizures, bloody vomiting, bloody diarrhea and shock, along with hypotension and tachycardia. Liver dysfunction and failure are possible in two to five days. The gut comes first, then the systemic signs and shock, and the liver last. The trap is the six to twenty four hour window, which holds two groups: the shock signs, and the hypotension with tachycardia. The antidote is deferoxamine, which chelates the circulating ferric iron.",
"TBL3-059": "Each antidote fits its poison's problem. Acetaminophen gets N-acetylcysteine, which replenishes the glutathione that conjugates N A P Q I. Toxic alcohols get ethanol or fomepizole, which block alcohol dehydrogenase so methanol and ethylene glycol are not turned into toxic acids. Benzodiazepines get flumazenil, and opioids get naloxone; both compete with the overdosed drug at its receptor. Digoxin gets digoxin specific antibody fragments, which bind digoxin for renal elimination. Sulfonylureas and meglitinides get octreotide, which inhibits insulin release from the pancreas and counters the hypoglycemia. The trap is swapping the two receptor antagonists, flumazenil and naloxone.",
"TBL3-060": "Where her key lists several treatments, each aims at the problem the overdose creates. Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers get the same six: atropine, calcium, glucagon, H I E T, vasopressors and intravenous fatty acid emulsion, against a slow heart and low blood pressure. Tricyclic antidepressants get intravenous fluids and dopamine or norepinephrine to maintain blood pressure, plus sodium bicarbonate for an elongated Q R S interval. Salicylates get supportive care and intravenous sodium bicarbonate for ion trapping. Iron gets deferoxamine, a chelator. Organophosphates get atropine against the acetylcholine excess and pralidoxime to free acetylcholinesterase. The trap is that sodium bicarbonate is listed for both tricyclic antidepressants and salicylates, and atropine for both beta blockers and organophosphates.",
"TBL3-061": "The six mechanisms are different kinds of action. N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione, which clears the toxic metabolite of acetaminophen. Ethanol or fomepizole inhibit alcohol dehydrogenase, the enzyme that would make the toxic metabolites. Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist of the benzodiazepine receptor, and naloxone is a competitive antagonist of opioid receptors; each blocks the receptor the overdosed drug is agonizing. Digoxin specific antibody fragments bind digoxin, which will be renally eliminated. Octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas, countering the hypoglycemia. The trap is pairing the two competitive antagonists with the wrong receptor.",
"TBL3-062": "Each mechanism here is tied to its poison. Deferoxamine chelates the circulating ferric iron. In organophosphate poisoning, pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase, and atropine is a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist against the acetylcholine excess. Sodium bicarbonate has two roles: for salicylates, renal ion trapping to enhance elimination, and for tricyclic antidepressants, it counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels. Glucagon in beta-blocker overdose increases cardiac cellular cyclic A M P to increase heart rate. The trap is the two bicarbonate mechanisms. Ion trapping needs a weak acid such as salicylate, while in tricyclic overdose the bicarbonate acts on the heart.",
"TBL3-063": "Six symptom pictures to tell apart. Acetaminophen shows nausea and vomiting with clinical signs of liver failure. Benzodiazepines show central nervous system depression with relatively normal vitals. Opioids show somnolence, coma, decreased heart and breath rates, and decreased pupils. Beta blockers show hypotension, bradycardia, altered mental status and respiratory depression. Calcium channel blockers show hypotension and bradycardia, but the patient is often alert. Digoxin shows bradycardia, arrhythmias, elevated serum potassium, and nausea and vomiting. The traps are the look-alike pairs: altered mental status marks the beta blocker against the alert calcium channel blocker patient, and falling vitals mark the opioid against the benzodiazepine.",
"TBL3-064": "Six more symptom pictures. Methanol metabolites cause anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ocular toxicity. Ethylene glycol metabolites crystallize in the microvasculature, and renal toxicity is common. Tricyclic antidepressants show tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation, seizures and an elongated Q R S interval. Salicylates show hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, then metabolic acidosis. Sulfonylureas and meglitinides show hypoglycemia. Organophosphate insecticides show acetylcholine excess, recalled as SLUDGE or DUMBELLS. The trap is the two toxic alcohols: the eyes and the acidosis belong to methanol, and the crystals and the kidney belong to ethylene glycol.",
"TBL3-065": "Her key raises a special consideration for six poisonings. Acetaminophen: the antidote loses effectiveness about eight hours after overdose, and yes, it is still given. Toxic alcohols: fomepizole is the stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor and has fewer adverse effects than ethanol. Benzodiazepines: flumazenil can cause withdrawal symptoms in a benzodiazepine-dependent patient. Beta blockers: the overdose may have altered mental status, unlike calcium channel blockers. Digoxin: after the antibody fragments, total digoxin, free and bound to Fab, is increased, so the level cannot guide more doses. Opioids: naloxone can trigger withdrawal in chronic opioid users. The trap is the two withdrawal answers, which belong to flumazenil and naloxone. She said a poisoning with no question has nothing really weird about it.",
"TBL3-068": "Her key names a reference for each poisoning. Eight cite the Marraffa article: acetaminophen, toxic alcohols, benzodiazepines, beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, digoxin, opioids, and sulfonylureas and meglitinides. Tricyclic antidepressants and salicylates cite Basic and Clinical Pharmacology, chapter fifty eight. Iron and organophosphate insecticides cite Pharmacotherapy, A Pathophysiologic Approach, thirteenth edition, chapter e six. The trap is assuming every poisoning cites Marraffa. The four that do not are the two treated with sodium bicarbonate, tricyclic antidepressants and salicylates, and iron and organophosphate insecticides."
});
